     1	# Artifact for "Crossover in Parametric Fuzzing"
     2	
     3	## Purpose
     4	
     5	This is the artifact for the paper "Crossover in Parametric Fuzzing" published at the International Conference on
     6	Software Engineering (ICSE) 2024.
     7	This artifact contains the materials needed to replicate the evaluation described in the paper,
     8	the source code for the parametric fuzzer Zeugma including an implementation of linked crossover,
     9	and the raw experiment data analyzed in the paper.
    10	The purpose of this artifact is to facilitate future research into structured fuzzing and to allow researchers to
    11	independently obtain the empirical results reported in the paper "Crossover in Parametric Fuzzing".
    12	
    13	We are claiming the "Available" and "Reusable" badges.
    14	This artifact is publicly accessible at https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.23688879.v1.
    15	This artifact includes source code, documentation, and detailed execution
    16	instructions for the tools and experiments used in the paper.
    17	We feel that this artifact will allow the research community to reuse and extend the methods and experiments
    18	presented in "Crossover in Parametric Fuzzing".
    19	
    20	## Provenance
    21	
    22	This artifact can be downloaded from https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.23688879.v1.
    23	
    24	## Data
    25	
    26	This artifact includes the raw experiment data for the results presented in "Crossover in Parametric Fuzzing".
    27	These included data files are described below.
    28	
    29	### heritability.csv
    30	
    31	The file "heritability.csv" contains inheritance rates and hybrid data for the heritability experiment.
    32	Each row reports results for a single child input and indicates the fuzzing subject that the input was created for, the
    33	type of crossover used to produce the child, the inheritance rate of the child, and whether the child was hybrid.
    34	Consider the following row:
    35	
    36	```
    37	subject,crossover_operator,inheritance_rate,hybrid
    38	...
    39	Rhino,Linked,0.817073,false
    40	```
    41	
    42	This row indicates that a child for "Rhino" created using "Linked" crossover had an inheritance rate of "0.817073" and
    43	was not a hybrid.
    44	There are 1,000 rows for each crossover operator for each subject.
    45	This dataset was used to create "Table 2: Heritability Metrics" of the paper.
    46	
    47	### saved.csv
    48	
    49	The file "saved.csv" contains data about the number of inputs that were saved to the corpus of campaigns for
    50	Zeugma-X.
    51	Each row indicates the identifier of the campaign, the subject the campaign was performed on, the total number of inputs
    52	saved over the full 3 hours of the campaign, and the number of inputs saved in the first 5 minutes of the campaign.
    53	Consider the following row:
    54	
    55	```
    56	campaign_id,subject,total_saved,saved_5m
    57	...
    58	264,Rhino,530,332
    59	```
    60	
    61	This row indicates that the campaign with the unique identifier "264" which was performed on "Rhino" saved a total of
    62	"530" inputs "332" of which were saved in the first 5 minutes of the campaign.
    63	We used this data to determine that, on average, over half of the inputs saved to a corpus at the end of a three-hour
    64	campaign were saved in the first five minutes.
    65	
    66	### coverage.csv
    67	
    68	The file "coverage.csv" contains JaCoCo branch coverage measurements for the fuzzing campaigns.
    69	Each row indicates the branch coverage recorded for a particular campaign at a certain time.
    70	Note that the measurement at 0 seconds is normally non-zero because times are computed relative to the first saved
    71	input.
    72	Times are in the format "D days HH:MM:SS[.UUUUUUUUU]", where D is days, HH is hours, MM is minutes, SS is seconds, and
    73	UUUUUUUUU is nanoseconds.
    74	
    75	Consider the following row:
    76	
    77	```
    78	campaign_id,fuzzer,subject,time,covered_branches
    79	...
    80	0,BeDiv-Simple,Ant,0 days 00:05:00,732
    81	```
    82	
    83	This row indicates that the campaign with the unique identifier "0" which was performed on "Ant" using the
    84	fuzzer "BeDiv-Simple" covered "732" branches by time "0 days 00:05:00".
    85	
    86	There are 1,001 measurements for each campaign: 1,000 measurements at equal time intervals starting at 0 seconds and
    87	ending at 3 hours, and 1 measurement at 5 minutes.
    88	This dataset was used to create "Figure 3: Branch Coverage Over Time" and "Table 3: Branch Coverage".
    89	
    90	### defects.json
    91	
    92	The file "defects.json" contains a JSON list of the unique defects detected in at least one
    93	campaign during the evaluation.
    94	This list was obtained by manually deduplicating detected failures.
    95	Each entry in the list indicates the unique identifier of the defect, the subject in which the defect was
    96	discovered, and a link to an issue in the subject's issue tracker for the defect.
    97	Consider the following entry:
    98	
    99	```JSON
   100	{
   101	  "subject": "Bcel",
   102	  "identifier": "B0",
   103	  "issue": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/BCEL-367"
   104	}
   105	```
   106	
   107	This entry describes defect "B0" which occurred in the subject "Bcel"
   108	and described in the issue at "https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/BCEL-367".
   109	
   110	### failures.json
   111	
   112	The file "failures.json" contains a JSON list of unique failures detected in at least one
   113	campaign during the evaluation.
   114	Each entry in the list indicates the subject in which the failure was detected, the Java type of the exception that was
   115	thrown, the stack trace of the thrown exception, and a list of associated defects that cause the failure to occur.
   116	Associated defects were determined by manually analyzing the failures.
   117	If the list of associated defects for an entry is empty, then the failure is not indicative of a genuine defect and
   118	the entry will contain a note explaining why this determination was made.
   119	
   120	Consider the following entry:
   121	
   122	```JSON
   123	{
   124	  "subject": "Rhino",
   125	  "trace": [
   126	    {
   127	      "declaringClass": "org.mozilla.javascript.TokenStream",
   128	      "fileName": "TokenStream.java",
   129	      "lineNumber": 1991,
   130	      "methodName": "ungetChar"
   131	    },
   132	    ...
   133	  ],
   134	  "type": "java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException",
   135	  "associatedDefects": [
   136	    "R0"
   137	  ]
   138	}
   139	```
   140	
   141	This entry describes a failure found in "Rhino" where the thrown exception is of
   142	type `java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` with the specified stack trace that is a manifestation of the
   143	defect "R0".
   144	
   145	### detections.csv
   146	
   147	The file "detections.csv" contains defect discovery times for the fuzzing campaigns.
   148	
   149	Each row indicates the time at which a campaign first discovered a defect.
   150	If no time is listed for a campaign on a defect, then that campaign did not discover that defect.


