   170	for the evaluation.
   171	Each directory within the archive contains the output for a single campaign.
   172	This output consists of four files: "coverage.csv", "failures.json", "summary.json", and "meringue.tgz".
   173	"coverage.csv" contains the branch coverage over time for the campaign.
   174	"failures.json" contains a list of deduplicated failures that were induced by at least one input saved during
   175	the campaign.
   176	"summary.json" contains information about the configuration used for the fuzzing campaign, e.g., the fuzzing
   177	target.
   178	"meringue.tgz" is a gzipped TAR archive of the [Meringue](https://github.com/neu-se/meringue) output for the campaign.
   179	
   180	## Setup
   181	
   182	1. Install Docker Engine version 23.0.0+.
   183	   Directions for installing Docker Engine are available on
   184	   the [Docker website](https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/).
   185	2. Download the archived Docker image "zeugma-artifact-image.tgz" from the artifact:
   186	   https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.23688879.v1.
   187	   This image can be recreated using the "Dockerfile" and source code archive "zeugma-main.zip" included with the
   188	   artifact.
   189	3. Load the Docker image by running: `docker load -i zeugma-artifact-image.tgz`.
   190	4. Start an interactive Docker container: `docker run -it -p 8080:80 zeugma-artifact bash`
   191	
   192	## Usage
   193	
   194	The following directions assume that you have started a docker container according to the directions provided above.
   195	All commands are run from the "home" directory of that docker container.
   196	
   197	### Running a Fuzzing Campaign
   198	
   199	To start a fuzzing campaign, run:
   200	
   201	```
   202	mvn -pl :zeugma-evaluation-tools \
   203	  meringue:fuzz meringue:analyze \
   204	  -P<SUBJECT>,<FUZZER> \
   205	  -Dmeringue.outputDirectory=<OUTPUT_DIRECTORY> \
   206	  -Dmeringue.duration=<DURATION>
   207	```
   208	
   209	Where:
   210	
   211	* \<SUBJECT\> is the fuzzing target: ant, bcel, closure, maven, nashorn, rhino, tomcat.
   212	* \<FUZZER\> is the fuzzer to be used: bedivfuzz-simple, bedivfuzz-structure, rlcheck, zest, zeugma-linked,
   213	  zeugma-none, zeugma-one_point, or zeugma-two_point.
   214	* \<OUTPUT_DIRECTORY\> is the absolute path of the directory to which campaign output files should be written.
   215	* \<DURATION\> is the maximum amount of time to execute the fuzzing campaign for specified in the ISO-8601 duration
   216	  format (e.g., "P2DT3H4M" represents 2 days, 3 hours, and 4 minutes).
   217	
   218	This command will first run a fuzzing campaign for the specified duration.
   219	Once this command has completed, the results of the campaign will be analyzed to collect coverage information
   220	using the [JaCoCo Java Code Coverage Library](https://www.eclemma.org/jacoco/) and information about exposed failures.
   221	After the analysis phase finishes, the output directory will contain the following files:
   222	
   223	```
   224	├── campaign
   225	│   ├── corpus
   226	│   │   └── *
   227	│   ├── failures
   228	│   │   └── *
   229	│   └── *
   230	├── jacoco
   231	│   └── jacoco.csv
   232	├── coverage.csv
   233	├── failures.json
   234	└── summary.json
   235	```
   236	
   237	The "campaign" directory stores data written by the fuzzer.
   238	This data will include a "corpus" directory containing coverage-revealing inputs saved during the fuzzing campaign and
   239	a "failures" directory containing failure-inducing inputs saved during the fuzzing campaign.
   240	
   241	When the fuzzing campaign is analyzed, inputs saved to the "corpus" and "failures" directories are run in order by
   242	timestamp to compute JaCoCo branch coverage over time, which is then saved to the file "coverage.csv".
   243	There are two columns in "coverage.csv": "time" and "covered_branches".
   244	The "time" column represents elapsed time in milliseconds since the beginning of the campaign, and the
   245	"covered_branches" column represents the number of branches covered in application classes as determined by JaCoCo.
   246	For example, if "coverage.csv" contained the row "100, 340" that would indicate that 340 branches were covered by inputs
   247	saved in the first 100 milliseconds of the campaign.
   248	
   249	The file "jacoco/jacoco.csv" is a JaCoCo-generated report detailing coverage in application classes after all the
   250	inputs saved to the "corpus" and "failures" directories have been run.
   251	
   252	The file "failures.json" contains a list of deduplicated failures that were induced by at least one input saved
   253	to "corpus" or "failures" directories.
   254	Failures are naively deduplicated using the top five elements of the failure's stack trace and the type of exception
   255	thrown (e.g., `java.lang.IndexOutOfBoundsException`).
   256	Each entry in "failures.json" will contain the top five elements (or fewer if the trace contains fewer than five
   257	elements) of the stack trace for the failure, the type of exception thrown, the elapsed time in milliseconds since the
   258	beginning of the campaign when the first input that induced the failure was saved, the message (as returned by
   259	`java.lang.Throwable#getMessage`) for the first instance of the failure, and a list of saved inputs
   260	that induced the failure.
   261	
   262	The file "summary.json" contains information about the configuration used for the fuzzing campaign, such as the Java
   263	options that were used and the fuzzing target.
   264	
   265	#### Example
   266	
   267	To test this command, run a one-minute fuzzing campaign on Mozilla Rhino using Zeugma-X:
   268	
   269	```shell
   270	mvn -pl :zeugma-evaluation-tools \
   271	  meringue:fuzz meringue:analyze \
   272	  -Prhino,zeugma-none \
   273	  -Dmeringue.outputDirectory=$(pwd)/results/zeugma-x/ \
   274	  -Dmeringue.duration=PT1M
   275	
   276	```
   277	
   278	Then, run a one-minute fuzzing campaign on Mozilla Rhino using Zeugma-Link (zeugma-linked):
   279	
   280	```shell
   281	mvn -pl :zeugma-evaluation-tools \
   282	  meringue:fuzz meringue:analyze \
   283	  -Prhino,zeugma-linked \
   284	  -Dmeringue.outputDirectory=$(pwd)/results/zeugma-link/ \
   285	  -Dmeringue.duration=PT1M
   286	
   287	```
   288	
   289	### Computing Heritability Metrics
   290	
   291	After one or more fuzzing campaigns have been completed using Zeugma-X (zeugma-none),
   292	you can compute the heritability of one-point, two-point, and linked crossover.
   293	First, create an input directory containing the campaigns to be used to compute crossover heritability.
   294	This input directory should contain one subdirectory for each campaign to be included in the report.
   295	Each of these subdirectories should contain the "summary.json" output file and the "campaign" directory from the
   296	fuzzing campaigns output (see ["Running a Fuzzing Campaign"](#Running-a-Fuzzing-Campaign) for information about these
   297	files).
   298	For example, the following is a valid structure for the input directory:
